@page
@model GitHubModel
@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "GitHub App";
    ViewData["Description"] = "A GitHub App that holds agent-authored pull requests with an unpinned action, a new dependency, a leaked key or a security exemption until a human looks.";
}

<section class="hero">
    <h1>Gate agent pull requests.</h1>
    <p class="lede">Install the App, add a three-line config with <code>"conclusion": "fail-on-warnings"</code>, and make <strong>Aibysitter review</strong> a required check. A pull request that adds an unpinned action, a new dependency, a leaked key or a security exemption then cannot merge until a human looks.</p>
    <p class="view-switch">
        <button type="button" class="button" data-copy="gate-config" data-copy-status="gate-copy-status" hidden>Copy</button>
        <span id="gate-copy-status" class="note" aria-live="polite"></span>
    </p>
    <pre id="gate-config"><code>{
  "conclusion": "fail-on-warnings"
}</code></pre>
    <p>Save it as <code>.github/aibysitter.json</code> on the default branch. Then require the <strong>Aibysitter review</strong> status check on that branch: <strong>Settings → Rules → Rulesets</strong>, or <strong>Settings → Branches</strong>.</p>
    <ul>
        <li><code>fail-on-errors</code> is the lighter setting: only Error findings block, such as a leaked key, a placeholder, a skipped or deleted test, a file outside <code>scope</code> or a committed <code>.env</code>.</li>
        <li><code>advisory</code> is the default and the trial mode: every finding is an annotation and nothing blocks.</li>
        <li>Bot dependency bumps (Dependabot, Renovate) block too, by design: a bot bumping a dependency is a change a human should sign off on.</li>
    </ul>
    <p><a class="button" href="@Aibysitter.Web.GitHub.GitHubAppLinks.InstallUrl">Install the GitHub App</a></p>
    <p>The App reads every pull request in a repository where it is installed. It posts a check named Aibysitter review, with an annotation on each line it flags. <a asp-page="/Privacy">What the App reads and keeps</a></p>
</section>

<section class="section">
    <h2>What it flags</h2>
    <ul class="marks">
        @foreach (var row in Model.Checks)
        {
            <li><a asp-page="/Rules/Rule" asp-route-id="@row.Doc.Id">@row.Doc.Id</a> @row.Doc.Summary <span class="note">@row.Severity</span></li>
        }
    </ul>
</section>

<figure class="terminal" aria-label="Example check run">
    <pre><code><span class="t-prompt">check</span> Aibysitter review  <span class="t-prompt">conclusion</span> failure
<span class="t-sum">4 findings (2 errors, 1 warning, 1 notice)</span>
<span class="t-loc">docs/notes.txt:1           </span> <span class="t-sev t-error">failure</span> <span class="t-rule">P004</span>  Outside declared scope (added): docs/notes.txt
<span class="t-loc">src/OrderService.cs:5      </span> <span class="t-sev t-error">failure</span> <span class="t-rule">P001</span>  Placeholder left in change: "YOUR_API_KEY"
<span class="t-loc">src/OrderService.cs:9      </span> <span class="t-sev t-warning">warning</span> <span class="t-rule">P002</span>  Stub left in change: "throw new NotImplementedException("
<span class="t-loc">.github/workflows/ci.yml:1 </span> <span class="t-sev t-info">notice </span> <span class="t-rule">P011</span>  CI config modified: .github/workflows/ci.yml</code></pre>
    <figcaption>Check run from a test pull request seeded with one example of each check, under <code>fail-on-warnings</code>.</figcaption>
</figure>

<section class="section">
    <h2>What we store</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>Code is fetched from GitHub for the duration of one review and discarded. Diffs, file contents and finding text are never written to disk, database or logs.</li>
        <li>Results go back to GitHub only: the check run, its annotations and summary, and the PR comment when <code>comment</code> is on.</li>
        <li>Stored per review: a daily count by conclusion. A queue file holding the repo, PR number and commit SHAs exists only while the review runs and is deleted when it finishes.</li>
        <li>Logs keep the repo owner and name, PR number, short SHA, delivery and check-run IDs, finding count and any error text for 14 days.</li>
    </ul>
</section>

<section class="section">
    <h2>Configuration</h2>
    <p>Optional. Add <code>.github/aibysitter.json</code> to the repository. The App reads it from the pull request's base branch, so a pull request cannot change how it is itself reviewed.</p>
    <pre><code>{
  "scope": ["src/**", "tests/**"],
  "conclusion": "fail-on-errors",
  "disable": ["P002"],
  "comment": true
}</code></pre>
    <table>
        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th>Key</th>
                <th>Values</th>
                <th>Default</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td><code>scope</code></td>
                <td>Path globs from the repository root. <code>**</code> matches any number of folders and must be a whole segment (<code>**/*.cs</code>, not <code>**.cs</code>); <code>*</code> and <code>?</code> match within one folder. Case-sensitive. Not supported: <code>{a,b}</code>, <code>[...]</code>, <code>!</code>, <code>\</code>. Turns on P004. Read only by P004; other checks review every changed file.</td>
                <td>Not set; P004 is off</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>conclusion</code></td>
                <td><code>advisory</code> reports findings as neutral (trial mode). <code>fail-on-warnings</code> fails the check when any Warning or Error finding exists; P014 fails only when a rule fires at Error. <code>fail-on-errors</code> fails the check when any Error finding exists. Info findings never fail the check. A review that stops on an error closes as "Review failed": failure under <code>fail-on-warnings</code> and <code>fail-on-errors</code>, neutral under <code>advisory</code>.</td>
                <td><code>advisory</code></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>disable</code></td>
                <td>Check IDs to skip, for example <code>["P002"]</code>; disabled checks do not run and show as disabled in the check summary. Rule IDs (<code>R001</code>–<code>R016</code>) skip that rule inside P014. <code>aibysitter-disable</code> comments in a rules file apply first.</td>
                <td>None</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>ignore</code></td>
                <td>Path globs skipped by content checks, or <code>{ "paths": [...], "checks": [...] }</code> to skip them for some checks only. P004, P008, P011, P013 and P014 still see every file. Up to 50 entries.</td>
                <td>None</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>comment</code></td>
                <td><code>true</code> posts one comment on the pull request with the summary table and up to 25 findings linked to their lines. Each new commit updates the same comment. No comment is created while there are no findings.</td>
                <td><code>false</code></td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>The config file is itself a changed file: a pull request that edits it outside the declared scope gets a P004 finding, and is reviewed with the base branch's version; the summary says so. Invalid entries fall back to their defaults, are listed in the check summary and are annotated on their line of the config file. Under <code>fail-on-warnings</code> or <code>fail-on-errors</code>, any config error fails the check.</p>
    <p><a asp-page="/GitHub/Config">Generate a config file</a></p>
</section>

<section class="section">
    <h2>Security model</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>Every webhook is verified against its HMAC-SHA256 signature; unsigned or mis-signed deliveries are rejected.</li>
        <li>The App acts as each installation with short-lived installation tokens. The App's private key stays on the server.</li>
        <li>Permissions: Checks (read and write), Pull requests (read and write; write is used only for the <code>comment</code> option), Contents (read). It cannot push, merge, or change settings.</li>
        <li>Checks are static text analysis. Code from the pull request is never run.</li>
        <li>It reads the pull request's head commit, and the config file from the base commit, with fixed caps: file contents for at most @Aibysitter.Web.GitHub.ReviewProcessor.MaxContentFetches changed files, @Aibysitter.Web.GitHub.ReviewProcessor.MaxUnchangedRulesFiles unchanged rules files and @Aibysitter.Web.GitHub.ReviewProcessor.MaxManifestFetches manifests per review; at most 2 MB of file content per review. Files over 100 KB, not UTF-8, or past the 2 MB limit are not read and are named in the check summary.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>What it reads, keeps and logs: <a asp-page="/Privacy">Privacy</a>.</p>
</section>

<section class="section">
    <h2>Install</h2>
    <p><a href="@Aibysitter.Web.GitHub.GitHubAppLinks.InstallUrl">Install the App</a>, pick the account or organization, and choose all repositories or selected ones. Installing needs owner or admin rights on the account.</p>
    <p><a href="@Aibysitter.Web.GitHub.GitHubAppLinks.InstallDocUrl">Step-by-step install guide</a></p>
    <p>To uninstall: <strong>Settings → Applications → Installed GitHub Apps → Aibysitter → Configure → Uninstall</strong> (organizations: <strong>Settings → GitHub Apps</strong>). Details: <a asp-page="/Privacy">Privacy</a>.</p>
</section>

@section Scripts {
    <script type="module" src="~/js/copy.mjs" asp-append-version="true"></script>
}
